p(18,14,"I don’t often speak of myself,” said John, “but as you are going",
 "John tells James how he lost his parents when he was young and was helped by the mistress and by Norman, and says that is why he is glad to help little Joe.",
 "John says he does not often speak about himself. But as James is going out into the world to manage for himself, John will tell him how he looks at these things. John was the same age as Joe when his father and mother died of a fever, within ten days of each other. They left him and his sister Nelly, who could not walk well, alone in the world with no relation to help them. He was a farm boy and did not earn enough to keep himself, and much less both of them. She would have had to go to the workhouse if it were not for their mistress. Nelly calls her her angel, and she is right to. The mistress found a room for Nelly with old Widow Mallet. She gave her sewing work when she could do it. When Nelly was ill she sent her dinners and many nice things, and she was like a mother to her. Then the master took John into the stable under old Norman, who was the coachman then. John had his food at the house, a bed in the loft, a suit of clothes and three shillings a week, so he could help Nelly. Norman could have said he was too old to be troubled with a raw boy from the plow, but he was like a father to John and took a great deal of trouble with him. When the old man died some years later, John took his place. Now he has the best pay and can save money for a rainy day or a sunny day, and Nelly is as happy as a bird. So John is not the man to look down on a little boy and upset a good, kind master. He will miss James very much, but they will get through. He says nothing is better than doing a kindness when it comes your way, and he is glad he can do it.",
 ["workhouse","needlework","raw","plow-tail","wages","rainy day","pull through"])
s(18,"she must have gone to the workhouse but for our mistress",
 "She would have had to go to the workhouse if it had not been for our mistress.",
 "'must have gone' here means 'would certainly have gone'; 'but for our mistress' means 'if it had not been for our mistress'.")
